int the hopes of.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The wind is mocking my efforts to light a fire!", "‘Mock’ certainly never signifies to loath. Its common signification is, to disappoint."], "quotes": [{"text": "\"It is the greene-ey'd Monster, which doth mocke / The meate it feeds on.\"", "reference": "c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Othello, the Moore of Venice”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene iii]:"}, {"text": "And Delilah ſaid vnto Samſon, hitherto thou haſt mocked me, and told me lies: tell me wherewith thou mighteſt be bound.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Judges 16:13, column 2:"}, {"text": "Why do I overlive? / Why am I mocked with death, and lengthened out / to deathless pain?", "reference": "1667, John Milton, “(please specify the page number)”, in Paradise Lost. […], London: […] [Samuel Simmons], and are to be sold by Peter Parker […]; [a]nd by Robert Boulter […]; [a]nd Matthias Walker, […], →OCLC; republished as Paradise Lost in Ten Books: […], London: Basil Montagu Pickering […], 1873, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "He will not […] / Mock us with his blest sight, then snatch him hence.", "reference": "1671, John Milton, “The Second Book”, in Paradise Regain’d. A Poem. In IV Books. To which is Added, Samson Agonistes, London: […] J[ohn] M[acock] for John Starkey […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "The French revolution indeed is a prodigy which has mocked the expectations both of its friends and its foes. It has cruelly disappointed the fondest hopes of the first, nor has it observed that course which the last thought that it would have pursued.", "reference": "1812, The Critical Review or, Annals of Literature, page 190:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(software engineering, transiti